<%# The document a ruact page renders into when `config.layout = "ruact"` (what
    `rails generate ruact:install` writes). It ships with the gem and is found by
    name through a view path the Railtie appends, behind the app's — so an
    `app/views/layouts/ruact.html.erb` wins over it. `rails generate ruact:layout`
    puts a copy there; once copied, that file is the app's and no longer changes
    when the gem does.

    What reaches the <head>: CSRF and CSP, the stylesheets Vite built for the
    client components, then the app's stylesheets named in
    `config.layout_stylesheets` — in that order, so the app's CSS loads last and
    wins ties. What does NOT: the app's JavaScript (importmap, Turbo, Stimulus)
    and anything else its own layout adds. The page that rendered the document
    owns its navigation; to bring more of the app in, copy this file.

    The <title> is the app's name on every page: a ruact view renders in its own
    pass, so nothing it sets can reach this layout. A copied layout can title
    pages however it likes. %>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title><%= (Rails.application.class.module_parent_name || Rails.application.class.name).to_s.titleize %></title>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
    <%= csrf_meta_tags %>
    <%= csp_meta_tag %>
    <%= ruact_head_assets %>
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag(*Ruact.config.layout_stylesheets) if Ruact.config.layout_stylesheets.any? %>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div id="root"></div>
    <%= ruact_js_assets %>
  </body>
</html>
